# What belongs in version control is the source, the tests and the
# documentation - about 1.6 MB. Everything below is either vendor binaries,
# reference data, or something a command can regenerate, and together it comes
# to roughly 11 GB. Git stores every version of every file it has ever seen
# forever, so a large file committed once is a large file committed always,
# even after it is deleted.
#
# Where an excluded thing is needed to build or run, docs/protocols say how to
# get it. Nothing here is required to read, test or review the code.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.venv/
venv/

# ------------------------------------------------- Vendor tools (3.9 GB)
# VSEARCH, the BLAST suite, the SRA toolkit and an embedded interpreter, one
# copy per platform. They are third-party releases downloaded whole, not
# something this project authors, and the build fetches them per platform.
# See docs/protocols/building-a-release.md.
/bin/

# -------------------------------------------- Reference libraries (5+ GB)
# A library is built, not written: hours of work from sources measured in
# gigabytes, and reproducible from docs/protocols/reference-data.md. This
# folder is usually a link to one kept elsewhere.
#
# Anchored with a leading slash. Without it this also matched
# src/reference/, and the first commit went in missing that whole package.
/reference/

# ------------------------------------------------------- Data and output
# Sequencing files under test, and everything any run has ever produced.
/test_data/
/test_output/
/output/
runs/
/cache/
/data/
*.log
*.fastq
*.fastq.gz
*.fq
*.fq.gz

# ...except the self-test's own reads, which are 12 KB, ship inside the
# application and are the thing it tests with. Excluding them leaves a
# manifest describing data that is not there, and a self-test that reports a
# broken installation on every fresh clone. Caught by tests/test_packaging.py.
!resources/selftest/*.fastq.gz

# ------------------------------------------------------------- Builds
/build/
/dist/

# ------------------------------------------------------ Editors and OS
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# ------------------------------------------------------------- Accidents
# Created by running a command with its example path left in place.
/path/
dist/installer/

# A library archive staged for upload. Around 700 MB, and the whole reason
# the library is a download rather than part of the release - so it must
# never end up in the history that release is built from.
/Zenodo Database/

# ------------------------------------------------------- Working, not shipped
# Kept on this machine and out of the repository. The attic holds superseded
# copies of modules that now live in src/, and test_config.yaml is a scratch
# settings file pointing at one developer's folders. Neither is referenced by
# anything; both are the first thing a visitor to the repository root sees.
/_attic/
/test_config.yaml

# ------------------------------------------- Downloadable content, staged here
# "Additional Content/" is a clone of the public content repository, and
# "Additional Content Subscriber/" is its paid counterpart. Both sit inside
# this folder for convenience and hold multi-gigabyte archives; neither
# belongs in this history. Note the nested .git: without these lines,
# `git add -A` records the clone as a gitlink to a commit no one else has.
/Additional Content/
/Additional Content Subscriber/

# ------------------------------------------ Not published, but not secret
# GPL-3 asks for the "Corresponding Source": what is needed to generate,
# install, run and modify the program. That is src/, resources/, installer/
# and the scripts that build it - all tracked above.
#
# These are separate works, and publishing them is a choice rather than an
# obligation. The choice here is not to. A public repository exists so that
# somebody can get the installer and, if they wish, the code; the test
# suite, the developer tooling and the engineering notes make that harder to
# find rather than easier, and the reasoning in them is ours.
#
# NONE OF THIS IS REPRODUCIBLE FROM THE REMOTE. A fresh clone is a correct
# and complete checkout containing none of it, so it must be backed up with
# Data/ and Writing/ rather than with the repository. tests/test_packaging.py
# checks both directions: that nothing here is tracked, and that none of it
# has quietly gone missing.
/tests/
/tools/
/dev-notes/
/docs/
# Orientation for whoever picks this up next. Withheld because almost
# every pointer in it leads into a folder above, and because one of its
# rules is about a drive on one particular machine.
/CLAUDE.md
